  "query_preview": "..\nValley fog, some locally dense, will reduce visibility early \nthis morning across CPA. Fog will gradually dissipate by 9-10AM. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Scattered strong to severe thunderstorms \npossible Thursday PM\n\nScattered thunderstorm development is expected Thursday\nafternoon ahead of a cold front across CPA. SPC expanded the \nlevel 2/5 severe t-storm risk southward to include the Lower \nSusquehanna Valley. WPC upgraded the ERO to level 2/4 for areas\nnear and to the east of US-15. Short term model guidance \ncontinues to highlight the eastern 1/3 of CPA with the most \nfavorable CAPE/shear profiles and highest pwats/slowest storm \nmotions. A predominantly multicellular storm mode is expected\nwith damaging winds the primary threat, however a few supercells\nmay also be possible. Higher FFGs could offset the flash flood\nrisk in the eastern zones to some extent, with the most\nsaturated soils and lowest FFG over the southwest quadrant of\nthe CWA. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nForecast viewing conditions for the lunar eclipse range from\nfair to poor Thursday night across central PA. The eclipse will\nbegin at 10:34 pm EDT and last for about 3 hours, ending at 1:52\nam EDT Friday. The worst view is most likely over the eastern \nhalf of CPA due to exiting storms and associated cloud cover.\nThe eclipse will take place during August's full moon, commonly\nreferred to as the Sturgeon Moon. It will also be the last full\nmoon of the summer with the next full moon set to rise a few\nnights after the autumnal (Fall) equinox on September 22nd. \n\nA drying trend is forecast Friday through the last weekend of\nAugust, thanks to high pressure building into the area behind\nthe cold front. This will likely setup a couple of foggy\nmornings. Rainfall looks fairly limited heading into the first\nweek of September; however despite the start of meteorological\nFall the summer is not over with temps expected to rise above\nthe historical average into the 80-90 degree range.",
